    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d6-a1">

                <pb facs="0097" n="5" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg5"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Briesen &amp; Knauth to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >New York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-02-08"><hi rendition="#italic">February 8,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Clients of ours, large exporters of ware
                    made in the United States to South American countries, especially to Brazil and
                    the Argentine Republic, complain to us that citizens of these countries are
                    permitted to bodily appropriate and register as their own the trade-marks and
                    symbols of United States citizens and to thus shut out importations from this
                    country or else force the importer to buy back from them the right which is
                    justly his own.</p>
                <p>We write to ask if any confirmation or denial of this alleged state of facts is
                    obtainable through your Department, and to request that the United States
                    diplomatic or consular officers in these countries be communicated with on this
                    subject, with the view to determining whether the practice complained of exists,
                    and whether, if it does, there is any adequate remedy.</p>
                <p>The favor of an answer is respectfully requested.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Briesen &amp;
                    Knauth.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
